{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to reverse its pioneering approach to drug decriminalization, as a new law comes into effect. This law will once again make it a crime to possess small amounts of hard drugs, such as fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Those caught with these substances will have the option to either face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option, known as a \"deflection program,\" aims to divert individuals from the criminal justice system and towards addiction and mental health services. However, this approach is not mandatory, and counties are encouraged to develop their own programs based on their resources. This has raised concerns that it may create a patchwork of policies, leading to unequal treatment of individuals.\n\n*** \nTo be eligible for the deflection program, individuals must meet specific criteria, such as having no other charges or warrants, and being medically stable. If they meet these requirements, officers will contact a mobile behavioral health outreach team to connect them with services. However, if the team cannot make contact within 30 minutes, the individual will be taken to jail.\n\n*** \nThe new law also introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public areas, such as parks. This move aims to make it easier for police to crack down on drug use in public. P.P.S As people of all age groups are affected by this law, it is essential to consider the broader implications for the country, where individuals may struggle to eat or go shopping due to the consequences of drug addiction, and the impact can be felt deeply, leaving a lasting impression on one's tongue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.004175516311079264}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.6931508779525757